<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Hawk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1767–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mahkatêwe-meshi-kêhkêhkwa, Black Sparrow Hawk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Black Hawk, Mahkatêwe-meshi-kêhkêhkwa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauk">Sauk</a>, was a Sauk leader and warrior in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwestern United States</a>. Not a hereditary civil chief, he earned his standing as a war leader. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> he fought for the British against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, hoping to push American settlers away from Sauk land, and in 1832 he led the British Band of Sauk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskwaki">Meskwaki</a> warriors in the Black Hawk War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. Captured and taken on a tour of eastern cities, he told his story to an interpreter; published in 1833, it was the first Native American autobiography printed in the United States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-hawk-sauk-leader.jpg" alt="Black Hawk" width="330" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Catlin_-_Múk-a-tah-mish-o-káh-kaik,_Black_Hawk,_Prominent_Sac_Chief_-_1985.66.2_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
